\DOC NONOVERLAPPING_TAC

\TYPE {NONOVERLAPPING_TAC : tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
{NONOVERLAPPING_TAC} proves a goal asserting that memory regions do not
overlap, deriving it from the non-overlap facts already in the assumption list.

\DESCRIBE
The tactic takes no arguments. It applies to a goal whose conclusion is a
memory non-overlap claim, in either the modular form or the abbreviated form,
and possibly a conjunction of several such claims; it uses the non-overlap
assumptions in the goal's assumption list, which may also contain many
unrelated assumptions about machine states, arithmetic bounds and quantified
lemmas. The target regions may be described by base pointers offset by word
arithmetic and by sizes involving symbolic arithmetic, and need not match any
assumption literally; claims about code regions given by a program counter and
length are also handled.
{
       A ?- t
     ============  NONOVERLAPPING_TAC
       (no goals)
}

\FAILURE
Fails when the conclusion is not of a non-overlap form it can handle: a plain
equation between two pointer terms fails in the internal conversion step, and a
disjunction of such an equation with a non-overlap claim fails because none of
the alternatives searched can be established. Both failing shapes were seen
with assumption lists that do contain non-overlap facts, so failure is driven
by the shape of the conclusion rather than by missing assumptions.

\COMMENTS
In all observed successful traces the tactic closes the goal, leaving zero
subgoals, so no transformed goal state is observed.

\ENDDOC
